{"hash": "eab0166a4516b631", "text": "So what does a C D actually do? It changes the dot for the session that ran it, and only that session. A child process cannot move its parent. If a program changes its own folder, the window you typed into keeps its own dot. That is the general rule, for every program that keeps a working directory. Python is the clearest case of a second list. Other tools have their own lookups too. Node, for example, resolves a required file from the calling file's folder, not the shell's. When you run python script dot P Y, the import list starts with that script's folder. With dash M or dash C, it starts with the current folder. Python 3.11 added dash P and an environment variable, P Y T H O N safe path, which stop Python putting those folders first. That story belongs to 05.02. A file path and an import path are two different lookups.", "alignment": {"characters": ["S", "o", " ", "w", "h", "a", "t", " ", "d", "o", "e", "s", " ", "a", " ", "C", " ", "D", " ", "a", "c", "t", "u", "a", "l", "l", "y", " ", "d", "o", "?", " ", "I", "t", " ", "c", "h", "a", "n", "g", "e", "s", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "d", "o", "t", " ", "f", "o", "r", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "s", "e", "s", "s", "i", "o", "n", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "r", "a", "n", " ", "i", "t", ",", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "o", "n", "l", "y", " ", "t", "h", "a", "t", " ", "s", "e", "s", "s", "i", "o", "n", ".", " ", "A", " ", "c", "h", "i", "l", "d", " ", "p", "r", "o", "c", "e", "s", "s", " ", "c", "a", "n", "n", "o", "t", " ", "m", "o", "v", "e", " ", "i", "t", "s", " ", "p", "a", "r", "e", "n", "t", ".", " ", "I", "f", " ", "a", " ", "p", "r", "o", "g", "r", "a", "m", " ", "c", "h", "a", "n", "g", "e", "s", " ", "i", "t", "s", " ", "o", "w", "n", " ", "f", "o", "l", "d", "e", "r", ",", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "w", "i", "n", "d", "o", "w", " ", "y", "o", "u", " ", "t", "y", "p", "e", "d", " ", "i", "n", "t", "o", " ", "k", "e", "e", "p", "s", " ", "i", "t", "s", " ", "o", "w", "n", "